# Sidecar L4DEL annotations for Theorem Proving in Lean 4 (tpil). The upstream sources are never modified.
# The book is written as Verso markup; the declarations below are the ones the scan lists out of its lean code blocks.

@file book/TPiL/PropositionsAndProofs.lean
@decl and_commut
-- L4DEL: title: An axiom: and is commutative, in a toy proof system
-- L4DEL: intuition: The book first models proofs as data (Proof p), and an axiom is just a constant of such a type.
-- L4DEL: step: Implies p q is defined as p → q, and Proof p is a structure with one field, proof : p, wrapping a proof of p.
-- L4DEL: step: axiom and_commut (p q : Prop) : Proof (Implies (And p q) (And q p)) postulates a proof of the commutativity of And with no argument for it.
-- L4DEL: step: With p q : Prop in scope, #check and_commut p q reports Proof (Implies (p ∧ q) (q ∧ p)).
-- L4DEL: step: This is a sketch of how a proof system could be represented; the book then moves to the real Lean treatment of propositions as types.
-- L4DEL: see: modus_ponens
-- L4DEL: term: axiom = a declaration whose type is asserted without any proof term; Lean takes it on trust.

@decl modus_ponens
-- L4DEL: title: Modus ponens as an inference rule on Proof
-- L4DEL: intuition: From a proof of p implies q and a proof of p, the rule produces a proof of q.
-- L4DEL: step: The block first repeats Implies and Proof from the previous example.
-- L4DEL: step: axiom modus_ponens (p q : Prop) : Proof (Implies p q) → Proof p → Proof q is stated as a function-typed axiom.
-- L4DEL: step: It is an axiom, so there is no proof term here; the rule is simply assumed.
-- L4DEL: see: and_commut
-- L4DEL: term: modus ponens = the inference rule that from p implies q and p concludes q.

@file book/TPiL/DependentTypeTheory.lean
@decl five
-- L4DEL: title: Example that fails: a numeral at a type that merely unfolds to Nat
-- L4DEL: intuition: def Natural := Nat makes Natural a new name, but numeral literals still need an OfNat Natural 5 instance that does not exist.
-- L4DEL: step: The book defines Natural as an alternative name for Nat with def Natural := Nat.
-- L4DEL: step: This code block is marked as an expected error: def five : Natural := 5 does not elaborate.
-- L4DEL: step: The recorded message says Lean failed to synthesize an instance of OfNat Natural 5, because numerals are polymorphic and the expected type is Natural.
-- L4DEL: step: The point is that Lean treats a defined name as equal to its unfolding, but not every name is unfolded in every context, such as instance lookup.

@file book/TPiL/Tactics.lean
@decl swap_pair
-- L4DEL: title: Tactics can define data: swapping a pair
-- L4DEL: intuition: The same tactic script that proves p and q implies q and p also builds a function that swaps the two components of a pair.
-- L4DEL: step: The type is α × β → β × α, a function type for products.
-- L4DEL: step: intro p introduces the argument; cases p splits the pair into its two components.
-- L4DEL: step: constructor <;> assumption builds the new pair, and for each of its two parts assumption finds the matching component in the context.
-- L4DEL: step: The book remarks that, up to variable names, this is identical to the proof for conjunction.
-- L4DEL: see: swap_sum

@decl swap_sum
-- L4DEL: title: Swapping the two sides of a sum type
-- L4DEL: intuition: cases splits on which side of the sum we are in, and each branch rebuilds the value on the opposite side.
-- L4DEL: step: The type is Sum α β → Sum β α.
-- L4DEL: step: intro p and cases p give one goal per constructor of Sum.
-- L4DEL: step: In the first branch apply Sum.inr; assumption wraps the left value on the right; in the second, apply Sum.inl; assumption does the reverse.
-- L4DEL: step: The book remarks that this matches the proof of commutativity of disjunction.
-- L4DEL: see: swap_pair
-- L4DEL: term: cases = the tactic that splits a value or hypothesis by its constructors and gives one goal for each.

@file book/TPiL/InductiveTypes.lean
@decl Weekday.next_previous
-- L4DEL: title: next undoes previous, by cases on the constructors
-- L4DEL: intuition: Weekday has seven constructors, so match gives seven cases, each true by computation.
-- L4DEL: step: Weekday is an inductive type with constructors sunday through saturday, and next and previous move one day forward and back.
-- L4DEL: step: theorem next_previous (d : Weekday) : next (previous d) = d is proved with match d with.
-- L4DEL: step: In each of the seven cases the two sides reduce to the same constructor, so the proof is rfl.
-- L4DEL: step: The book follows this with a shorter version of the proof.
-- L4DEL: term: inductive type = a type generated freely by a list of constructors, such as the seven weekdays.

@decl Weekday.Weekday.add_comm
-- L4DEL: title: First attempt at commutativity of Nat addition (contains sorry)
-- L4DEL: intuition: Induct on the second argument; one equation in the step is not yet proved and is left as sorry.
-- L4DEL: step: This is a work-in-progress block in the book: the last calc step ends with := sorry, so Lean accepts it only with a warning.
-- L4DEL: step: Nat.recOn with motive fun x => m + x = x + m does induction on n; the base case m + 0 = 0 + m is proved with Nat.zero_add and Nat.add_zero.
-- L4DEL: step: In the inductive step a calc rewrites m + succ n to succ (m + n), then to succ (n + m) using the induction hypothesis.
-- L4DEL: step: The missing step succ (n + m) = succ n + m is what the book proves next as succ_add, then plugs in to replace the sorry.
-- L4DEL: see: Weekday.Weekday.succ_add
-- L4DEL: term: induction hypothesis = in the inductive step, the assumption that the property holds for n, used to prove it for succ n.

@file book/TPiL/QuantifiersEquality.lean
@decl divides_trans
-- L4DEL: title: Divisibility is transitive, so calc can chain it
-- L4DEL: intuition: Compose the two witnesses by multiplying them, which is the witness for the combined divisibility.
-- L4DEL: step: divides x y is defined as ∃ k, k*x = y.
-- L4DEL: step: let ⟨k₁, d₁⟩ := h₁ and let ⟨k₂, d₂⟩ := h₂ unpack the two witnesses and their equations.
-- L4DEL: step: The new witness is k₁ * k₂, and the equation is proved by rewriting with Nat.mul_comm, Nat.mul_assoc, d₁ and d₂.
-- L4DEL: step: The book uses it to build instance Trans divides divides divides, which teaches calc about this relation.
-- L4DEL: see: divides_mul

